WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.940
Have you ever needed to both start
and end a knitted piece with a hem?

2
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:12.280
Scarves and blankets are good
examples for using this technique.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.220
Here is an overview of knitting
an item with a reverse hem.

4
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:23.660
You'll start with a normal hem
and knit the body of your item.

5
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.700
Towards the end of your knitting,
you're going to knit a marker row.

6
00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.380
We'll talk about a couple
different methods of marker rows.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.180
You'll knit the public side of the hem.

8
00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:38.020
You'll knit a turning row,
knit the private side of the hem,

9
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.580
and then hang the hem
from that marked row and bind off.

10
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.940
One of the simplest marking rows is
to simply use a contrasting color sewing

11
00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:54.260
thread and knit that in the row
that you want to mark.

12
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.660
You can use the contrasting color to pick

13
00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.260
up the hem and remove
that sewing thread easily.

14
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.240
When you're done.

15
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.800
Try weaving ravel cord over
and under every other needle.

16
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.820
You can then remove the ravel cord.

17
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.410
Also, after you've picked up your hem.

18
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:20.700
Try slipping every other needle
for a marker row.

19
00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:26.490
For this example,
we are adjusting our tension of our hem.

20
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.460
I'm assuming that you've already done
the first part of your hem,

21
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:35.020
so just repeat whatever tension changes
you did on the first part of your hem

22
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.160
on the reversed hem.

23
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.780
You'll knit the body of your piece,
knit the public side of your hem,

24
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.260
knit a turning row,
and the private side of your hem.

25
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.100
Now you put that marker row to work.

26
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:58.210
You can pick up every stitch
or every other stitch.

27
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.850
We just want to get
a nice join for the hem.

28
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.660
As always in knitting, you've got some
choices in binding off this reverse hem.

29
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.170
After you hang the marked row,
you could use a tapestry needle or

30
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.450
a double-eye needle and just run
a tail of yarn through the stitches.

31
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.020
This will secure this hem very well.

32
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:26.660
If you do this neatly,
the hem lays very, very flat.

33
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.610
One suggestion is to knit the last row

34
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.680
more tightly to reduce
the size of the loops.

35
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.300
Another idea is for a really fast finish,

36
00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:44.760
is to just bind off those stitches using
whatever bindoff method you'd like.

37
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:53.760
Whatever method you choose, the hems will
be beautiful on both edges of your item.

38
00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.180
Thank you for watching
Machine Knitting Now Learning Library has

39
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:05.780
hundreds of techniques on a variety
of subjects at all skill levels.

40
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:11.800
Please consider a subscription and let us
help you be a better machine knitter.

